How to Improve Your Chances of Passing the Driving Test First Time
Many learners want to pass on their first attempt. Although no instructor can sincerely promise a specific outcome, a systematic preparation plan can help you approach the test with more confidence.

Practice a lot

Long breaks between classes can make it harder to retain techniques and build confidence. Try to keep a routine so you have time to learn and practice each skill where possible.

Your instructor can guide you on a suitable speed based on your expertise and progress.

Know the mistakes that you repeat

Write down two or three things you need to improve after each lesson. These could be mirror checks, approach speed, clutch control, or observation at crossroads.

Review the list before your next session so that you come to it with a clear aim in mind.

Know the road rules

Practical driving and theory go hand in hand. You make better decisions when you understand signs, road markings, right-of-way rules, and safe driving concepts.

Don’t rely solely on informal online recommendations; use current official RSA learning resources.

When you are ready, take a mock test

A simulated exam can reveal issues that don’t usually come up during casual practice. Use the data to improve teaching rather than to forecast the real exam.

Get ready for test day

Review your appointment information, learner permit, relevant documents, and test car arrangements ahead of time. Ensure that all of your EDT requirements have been completed and recorded where appropriate.

The RSA’s driving test eligibility guidance addresses the applicable requirements.

